WEBVTT

00:00:00.792 --> 00:00:03.750
This is a Final Project
Pitch Film created by

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:08.000
Ethan Gomes. The project is
called DataSentry.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.167
Nothing is built yet;

00:00:09.458 --> 00:00:11.083
everything here is a plan.

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:14.458
The question isn't whether Claude
can write data checks.

00:00:14.792 --> 00:00:17.125
It's whether anyone can
measure what they miss.

00:00:17.292 --> 00:00:19.417
Three terms. A data check:

00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:21.625
a rule a table must pass.

00:00:21.833 --> 00:00:25.000
A planted defect: a mistake
put into a copy

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:26.417
of the data on purpose.

00:00:26.792 --> 00:00:27.750
And a catch rate:

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:30.625
the share of planted defects
the checks find.

00:00:31.667 --> 00:00:34.250
DataSentry will be a workflow,
built in Claude Code,

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:37.708
that writes data checks for a
table, then scores

00:00:37.708 --> 00:00:40.833
those checks by planting
defects they have to catch.

00:00:40.917 --> 00:00:42.958
When it's done, it will be a public GitHub

00:00:42.958 --> 00:00:44.500
repo with three parts:

00:00:44.833 --> 00:00:48.458
a check writer, a defect
planter, and a scorecard

00:00:48.458 --> 00:00:50.208
of what was caught and what was missed.

00:00:50.375 --> 00:00:53.250
It will start from public
data, for example one

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:56.250
month of New York City yellow
taxi trips, about

00:00:56.375 --> 00:01:01.375
ten thousand rows. Pick-up time,
drop-off time, passengers,

00:01:01.625 --> 00:01:02.750
distance, and fare.

00:01:02.792 --> 00:01:05.583
Claude Code will see only
the column names and

00:01:05.583 --> 00:01:09.875
types, and write checks in
pandera, an open-source Python

00:01:09.875 --> 00:01:12.167
library for testing data frames.

00:01:12.708 --> 00:01:14.583
It will never see the list of defects.

00:01:14.833 --> 00:01:17.875
Separately, a plain Python
script will copy the clean

00:01:17.875 --> 00:01:20.042
sample and break it five ways:

00:01:20.625 --> 00:01:25.042
duplicates, missing values,
impossible numbers like a negative

00:01:25.042 --> 00:01:28.625
fare, a drop-off before its
pick-up, and the wrong

00:01:28.625 --> 00:01:29.208
type.

00:01:29.291 --> 00:01:31.166
Then every check runs twice.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.625
On the clean copy, it must pass.

00:01:33.875 --> 00:01:36.125
On the broken copy, it must fail.

00:01:36.625 --> 00:01:38.708
Out comes a catch rate for each kind of

00:01:38.708 --> 00:01:40.916
defect, with every miss written down.

00:01:41.041 --> 00:01:42.208
Why would anyone care?

00:01:42.666 --> 00:01:45.958
I looked at two tools data
teams already use.

00:01:45.958 --> 00:01:50.208
First, dbt. Its built-in tests
check exactly what you

00:01:50.208 --> 00:01:54.250
write: unique, not null, accepted
values, relationships.

00:01:54.583 --> 00:01:56.625
The second is Great Expectations.

00:01:57.166 --> 00:02:01.666
Its paid GX Cloud has a feature,
ExpectAI, that suggests

00:02:01.666 --> 00:02:03.958
checks for you to review and approve.

00:02:04.541 --> 00:02:07.458
So AI-suggested checks already exist.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:10.333
What I didn't find, in what I read, was

00:02:10.333 --> 00:02:12.875
a way to measure how good
those checks are.

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:16.666
DataSentry will tell a data
engineer which bad rows

00:02:16.666 --> 00:02:18.291
their checks would let through.

00:02:18.416 --> 00:02:21.541
Who opens it? A hiring manager for a data

00:02:21.541 --> 00:02:24.958
engineering role. In the first
minute, they'll run one

00:02:24.958 --> 00:02:27.666
command on a broken file
and see each defect

00:02:27.666 --> 00:02:29.708
type: caught, or missed.

00:02:29.916 --> 00:02:30.875
What would it prove?

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.291
Four skills from this class:

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:36.458
a prompt contract for each
check, an agent loop

00:02:36.458 --> 00:02:40.916
that writes, runs, and revises,
verification against a clean

00:02:40.916 --> 00:02:43.750
copy, and evaluation by catch rate.

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:46.916
Once it's built, a reviewer
could fairly conclude that

00:02:46.916 --> 00:02:49.999
I test an AI's output
instead of trusting it.

00:02:50.374 --> 00:02:52.291
The evidence will be in the repo:

00:02:52.666 --> 00:02:55.666
the scorecard, the log of
every round, and the

00:02:55.666 --> 00:02:57.374
prompts behind each check.

00:02:57.457 --> 00:03:01.499
In scope: one table, five defect
types, one scorecard.

00:03:01.707 --> 00:03:03.207
Out of scope, on purpose:

00:03:03.624 --> 00:03:05.207
orchestration, so no Airflow.

00:03:05.666 --> 00:03:08.874
First to run: one defect, one
check, one printed

00:03:08.874 --> 00:03:11.041
line. If it prints, it ran.

00:03:11.165 --> 00:03:12.249
What could sink it?

00:03:12.624 --> 00:03:14.082
Checks that are too loose.

00:03:14.374 --> 00:03:17.499
A check that passes everything
catches nothing, and the

00:03:17.499 --> 00:03:19.249
score would still look fine.

00:03:19.582 --> 00:03:22.124
So the clean copy and the broken copy are

00:03:22.124 --> 00:03:24.082
both required, every time.

00:03:24.207 --> 00:03:26.707
Why this project? It will help me land a

00:03:26.707 --> 00:03:29.749
data job, like a Data
Engineer or Data Analyst

00:03:29.749 --> 00:03:33.374
role. It will highlight the
skills I'm working on:

00:03:33.707 --> 00:03:37.499
Python, pandas, pandera, and
Claude Code in a loop

00:03:37.499 --> 00:03:39.332
that fixes its own checks.

00:03:39.415 --> 00:03:43.582
Where this stands today,
October fourteenth, 2026.

00:03:43.582 --> 00:03:46.748
What exists: this pitch, a
written brief, and my

00:03:46.748 --> 00:03:48.332
notes on the two tools.

00:03:48.707 --> 00:03:50.123
What doesn't exist yet:

00:03:50.332 --> 00:03:52.915
the code, the defect
planter, and any score.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:55.540
That's DataSentry. I'm Ethan Gomes.

00:03:55.707 --> 00:03:56.665
Thanks for watching.
